nodecode-iwork is a rewrite of Arkanji's Arkanji/iwork-studio:

  https://github.com/Arkanji/iwork-studio
  commit 551107ae937960c785d3bf8b180c573515b6d359
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Arkanji (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the verbs a user reaches for most, in Common Lisp and without Python. The
original is a manifest that pulls a Python MCP server from PyPI and drives Apple's apps
through osascript; this cell writes the JavaScript for Automation itself and runs it with the
osascript macOS ships, so it installs nothing and executes nothing it did not ship. Kept are
what a user of the original gets: the same job on the same files - read a Numbers table,
set a cell, list and edit a Keynote deck's slides, export, and undo any write - a fence the
operator sets (the original's IWORK_STUDIO_ROOTS, here the `folders` setting), a backup
before every write, the file re-read and compared with what was asked before the write is
trusted, and a restore that puts a file back byte for byte.

Left out, because the rewrite is a cell and not a 65-tool server plus a Python library: the
pure-Python route that reads and edits .numbers, .key and .pages files without the app (and
with it the chart-aware rewriting, the byte-exact Arabic round-trip guarantee and reading a
file on any machine), formulas and recalculation, number formats, borders, merges and cell
styling, design kits and brand extraction, CSV import, templates, thumbnails, presenter
notes, themes, layouts, transitions, images, charts, tables on slides, slideshow control,
rendered-slide review and the PDF text and format checks. Where the original re-read the
file with its own parser, this cell asks the app what it kept and puts the backup back when
the two disagree.
